Apple tested end-to-end encryption (E2EE) for <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/rcs/">RCS</a> messages exchanged between iPhone and Android users in the iOS 26.4 beta, but Apple made it clear the functionality was not going to launch in the iOS 26.4 update.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/03/RCS-Feature-1.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-994897" /><br/>
E2EE for &zwnj;RCS&zwnj; was removed before iOS 26.4 was released, but the feature is back in the iOS 26.5 beta as Apple continues testing it.<br/>
<br/>
In the Messages section of the Settings app, the End-to-End Encryption toggle is back in the &zwnj;RCS&zwnj; options, and like before, it is turned on by default.<br/>
<br/>
End-to-end encryption ensures that messages sent between devices cannot be intercepted and read by a third party. Right now, &zwnj;RCS&zwnj; messages sent between Android and iPhone users do not have E2EE, but &zwnj;RCS&zwnj; messages between Android users do. iMessages exchanged between iPhone users have always had end-to-end encryption.<br/>
<br/>
